# Implement the browser-session Proton adapter

## Goal

Provide the primary Proton adapter behind the provider-neutral mail port using
the authenticated session established by the visible login flow.

## Required work

- Use the stable authenticated protocol boundary accepted by the architecture
  decision; isolate any unavoidable browser interaction behind one adapter.
- Detect account and server capabilities instead of assuming a plan or rollout.
- Implement bounded timeouts, cancellation, retry policy, backoff, concurrency,
  session refresh, and structured provider error conversion.
- Convert Proton messages, conversations, labels, drafts, events, and
  attachments into provider-neutral domain types.
- Preserve end-to-end cryptographic behavior required to read and send mail;
  never log decrypted content or key material.
- Fail closed when Proton changes an observed contract, rather than silently
  returning incomplete or mis-threaded data.

## Acceptance criteria

- Every capability advertised by the MCP server has a tested implementation or
  is omitted through capability negotiation.
- The adapter survives session refresh, pagination, rate limiting, transient
  disconnects, and a controlled incompatible-response fixture.
- Provider-specific types, URLs, cookies, and cryptographic material do not
  cross into the capability domain or public tool schemas.
- Browser automation is not used as brittle click-and-scrape mail transport.
- A live opt-in account can perform the complete advertised read, draft, send,
  reversible-action, attachment, and mailbox-event workflow through this path.

## Evidence

- Adapter contract and protocol-fixture tests
- Capability-negotiation matrix
- Session-refresh and provider-change regression tests
- Redacted live-account adapter transcript
